 ## Background
-The concept of removing interior polygons to improve rendering performance originated in the context of voxel-based procedural terrain generation, specifically referencing implementations similar to Minecraft [S1, unofficial]. The initial observation was that brute-force instantiation of cube models resulted in the engine needlessly rendering 12 triangles (6 sides) when only 2 triangles (1 side) were visible, leading to the optimization of reconstructing cubes from triangles and removing sides adjacent to solid neighbors [S1, unofficial]. This approach was further optimized by combining the meshes of all cubes into a single big mesh called a 'chunk' [S1, unofficial].
+The technique originated with the optimization of voxel-based procedural terrain generation, specifically in the context of games like Minecraft. Early implementations used brute-force instantiation of cube models, which resulted in rendering unnecessary internal polygons (12 triangles per cube side vs. 2 triangles for a visible face) [S1, unofficial]. To improve performance, a class was added to reconstruct cubes from triangles and check for solid neighbors, removing sides adjacent to them [S1, unofficial]. The meshes of all cubes were then combined into a single big mesh called a 'chunk' to further optimize implementation, though this introduced vertex count limits (e.g., ~65,000 vertices in Unity) [S1, unofficial].
 
